---
import DocLayout from '../../../../layouts/DocLayout.astro';
import TechBadge from '../../../../components/TechBadge.astro';
import AttestreeDiagram from '../../../../components/diagrams/AttestreeDiagram.astro';
import docNavs from '../../../../data/doc-navs.en.json';
import { useTranslations } from '../../../../i18n/utils';
import type { Lang } from '../../../../i18n/utils';
import { getRelativeLocaleUrl } from 'astro:i18n';

const lang = (Astro.currentLocale || 'de') as Lang;
const t = useTranslations(lang);
const nav = docNavs['attestree'];

const tech = ['.NET', 'winget', 'in-toto / SLSA', 'CycloneDX', 'Sigstore', 'PostgreSQL', 'Docker', 'Hyper-V / Proxmox', 'Astro'];

const projectSchema = JSON.stringify({
  "@context": "https://schema.org",
  "@type": "SoftwareApplication",
  "name": "Attestree",
  "applicationCategory": "DeveloperApplication",
  "operatingSystem": "Windows",
  "url": "https://attestree.com",
  "description": "Deployment control plane for Windows fleets that attests every winget package at ingest: sandboxed detonation, an observed SBOM and a signed in-toto statement before it rolls out in rings from a fenced feed.",
  "author": {
    "@type": "Person",
    "name": "Marc Borer",
    "url": "https://marc-borer.ch"
  },
  "programmingLanguage": ["C#", "TypeScript"],
  "keywords": "software supply chain, winget, attestation, SBOM, in-toto, SLSA, provenance, Windows fleet, ring deployment, private winget source"
});
---

<DocLayout title="Attestree — Attested winget for Windows Fleets" description="Case study of Attestree: a deployment control plane that detonates every winget package at ingest, gives it an observed SBOM and signs it before it rolls out in rings from a fenced feed." ogImage="/og/attestree.png" sidebarTitle={nav.title} sidebarItems={nav.items}>
  <article class="case-study">
    <a href={getRelativeLocaleUrl(lang, '/projects/')} class="back-link">
      <svg width="14" height="14" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M19 12H5M11 18l-6-6 6-6"/></svg> {t('projects.backToProjects')}
    </a>

    <header class="case-header">
      <h1 class="case-title">Attestree</h1>
      <p class="case-subtitle">Attested winget for Windows fleets: every package is detonated, given an SBOM and signed before it rolls out.</p>
      <div class="tags">
        {tech.map((name) => <TechBadge name={name} />)}
      </div>
      <p class="case-meta">
        <span>{t('projects.role')}: founder, architecture and implementation</span>
        <span>{t('projects.period')}: 2026 – present</span>
        <span>{t('projects.site')}: <a href="https://attestree.com" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">attestree.com</a></span>
        <span>
          <svg width="14" height="14" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.75" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><rect x="4" y="11" width="16" height="10" rx="1.5"/><path d="M8 11V7a4 4 0 0 1 8 0v4"/></svg>
          Platform code private, installer shim Apache-2.0
        </span>
      </p>
    </header>

    <section class="case-section">
      <h2>Problem</h2>
      <p>
        Most Windows shops install winget packages straight from the public source and discover
        problems only after a scanner runs against an endpoint days later. By then the artifact is on
        the whole fleet, SBOMs are an afterthought, attestations are missing, and the supply-chain
        story is “we trust Microsoft’s CDN”. The usual tools scan deployed binaries after the fact,
        and app-patching tools publish third-party updates into Intune or ConfigMgr without asserting
        the integrity of the apps they carry. Nobody checks the catalog and distribution layer before
        deploy.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="case-section">
      <h2>Approach</h2>
      <p>
        Attestree reverses the order: <em>winget install, with a receipt.</em> With a detonation host
        attached (Hyper-V or Proxmox/KVM), each winget version you ingest is installed for real in an
        isolated VM. Its actual filesystem and registry footprint is captured, not inferred from a
        manifest. Syft builds a CycloneDX SBOM of what the installer put on disk, and the platform
        signs an in-toto statement about the installer’s SHA-256 that can be verified offline. Only
        then is the version promoted to a ring.
      </p>
      <p>
        Endpoints are fenced to the operator’s own attested winget feed: a fenced endpoint installs
        exactly the pinned SHA-256 and rejects anything else. When someone asks the source for a
        package that has not been admitted, the feed declines cleanly and records the demand, so what
        to admit next is measured pull rather than guesswork.
      </p>
    </section>

    <AttestreeDiagram />

    <section class="case-section">
      <h2>Architecture</h2>
      <p>
        The system has two surfaces. A control plane with an endpoint agent runs ingest, attestation
        and deployment. The public front door is the website and the open-core Community Edition.
      </p>

      <h3>Ingest gate</h3>
      <p>
        Every package goes through fetch from the public winget source, installer-signature and
        publisher-continuity checks, sandboxed detonation, an observed CycloneDX 1.6 SBOM and a policy
        decision. The result is signed as an in-toto statement (ECDSA P-256). winget is managed and
        attested end to end today; Chocolatey and Scoop are inventoried and not deployed until they
        can be attested. Further package managers and MSI/MST transforms are on the roadmap.
      </p>

      <h3>Fenced feed, groups × rings</h3>
      <p>
        Endpoints install from the operator’s own spec-compliant winget REST source, pinned to the
        approved SHA-256. PC groups decide <em>what</em> a device runs; rings (canary → pilot → broad →
        all) decide <em>which version</em>. Rollback returns to any earlier version. A failing rollout
        produces a rollback proposal that the operator confirms, rather than the system acting on its
        own.
      </p>

      <h3>Unattended updates with a halt queue</h3>
      <p>
        An optional pipeline ingests new winget versions, detonates them and promotes them ring by
        ring. It ships disabled and is armed deliberately. It halts for review when a publisher’s
        signing certificate changes or an installer arrives unsigned, which is exactly the signal a
        hijacked release produces.
      </p>

      <h3>Control plane stack</h3>
      <ul class="feature-list">
        <li><strong>.NET</strong>: a Linux container as the control plane; a Native AOT installer shim and a self-updating endpoint agent, rolled out through the same rings</li>
        <li><strong>PostgreSQL and Docker</strong>: one server image with Postgres; the Community Edition runs on Docker Compose with no cloud dependency</li>
        <li><strong>Detonation hosts</strong>: Hyper-V or Proxmox/KVM, with a golden image the operator builds from their own ISO</li>
        <li><strong>Open formats and signatures</strong>: in-toto, SLSA v1, CycloneDX 1.6 (Syft); Sigstore-cosign-signed images and Authenticode-signed Windows binaries</li>
        <li><strong>Fleet CVE index</strong>: self-hosted, matching observed inventory against cvelistV5 and CISA KEV, with an offline bundle for air-gapped hosts</li>
      </ul>
    </section>

    <section class="case-section">
      <h2>Key technical decisions</h2>

      <div class="decision">
        <h3>Attest at ingest, not scan after install</h3>
        <p>
          The structural wedge: Attestree owns the catalog and distribution layer and checks before
          deploy. Without pre-deploy attestation, “we inventory it” is just a prettier
          <code>winget list</code>.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="decision">
        <h3>An observed SBOM, not a declared one</h3>
        <p>
          The SBOM comes from what the installer actually did in the sandbox, not from the publisher’s
          manifest. An ingest SBOM is an observation, and that is what makes it worth signing.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="decision">
        <h3>Three enforcement points</h3>
        <p>
          In a Windows fleet the endpoint is the load-bearing enforcement point. Policy is therefore
          not a single pipeline check: the ingest gate blocks before the catalog, reconciliation
          detects drift from declared state, and the agent re-verifies signature, hash and policy at
          install.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="decision">
        <h3>A person confirms the destructive step</h3>
        <p>
          Automation proposes, people approve. Rollback proposals need confirmation and ship disabled.
          The update pipeline stops in a review queue rather than guessing when a publisher’s signing
          identity changes.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="decision">
        <h3>Open-core with an enforced licence boundary</h3>
        <p>
          The installer shim and its IPC contract are Apache-2.0; the platform is closed source. The
          licence boundary is enforced mechanically in the build, so closed-source code cannot be
          relicensed by accident. The free Community Edition self-hosts on Docker Compose, ships as
          Sigstore-cosign-signed images and is in early access. Commercially there is a dedicated
          instance in the customer’s own Azure subscription, deliberately no shared multi-tenant SaaS.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="case-section">
      <h2>Way of working</h2>
      <p>
        Releases are cut on a daily cadence as soon as the CI gates on the main branch are green; a
        release without an agent change starts no agent rollout. Architecture decisions are kept as
        decision records, and the website copy is re-checked against the actual product on every
        release: claims the product does not back are removed rather than softened.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="case-section">
      <h2>Public site</h2>
      <p>
        The site at attestree.com is a separate, fully public surface: a static Astro build with
        Tailwind and MDX, deployed to Cloudflare Pages with a preview deploy per PR. The waitlist runs
        as a same-origin Cloudflare Pages Function with D1 in an EU jurisdiction and Turnstile
        verification. The build-security baseline covers SHA-pinned actions, least-privilege tokens, a
        committed lockfile, Dependabot, an <code>npm audit</code> CI gate and <code>security.txt</code>.
      </p>
    </section>
  </article>
  <script type="application/ld+json" set:html={projectSchema} />
</DocLayout>
